Evidence-Intersection Mapping for Mars: A Provenance-Preserving Computational Layer for Cross-Modal Scientific Attention Ranking

Theoretical PhD Proposal — Final Version

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Development Model: Human–AI Collaborative Research
Date: September 2026

---

Abstract

Mars has been observed by orbital and surface assets for over five decades, producing a heterogeneous archival corpus exceeding 1.2 petabytes across imaging, spectroscopy, topography, radar, atmospheric, and geochemical modalities. Contemporary autonomous targeting systems (AEGIS) have demonstrated that computational selection can increase scientific productivity under operational constraints, achieving >93% target-acquisition success on Curiosity relative to a 24% baseline. However, a distinct problem remains unaddressed: given the existing corpus of heterogeneous observations, how can geographically localized regions be systematically identified where the configuration of available evidence — its coverage, modality diversity, cross-modal agreement, discrepancy, and unresolved context — indicates that further scientific attention may be warranted?

This proposal introduces Evidence-Intersection Mapping (EIM), a lightweight, provenance-preserving computational component operating at the post-acquisition evidence-synthesis layer. EIM does not perform visual anomaly detection (VAD), nor does it replace human-driven GIS analysis or multi-criteria decision-making (MCDM) suitability assessment. Instead, it constructs region-level evidence profiles from heterogeneous archival observations and produces Candidate Scientific Attention Zones (CSAZs): ranked regions where the evidence configuration itself — not any single anomaly or measurement — justifies human scientific inspection.

The central epistemic constraint is preserved:

\text{Observation} \neq \text{Anomaly} \neq \text{Discrepancy} \neq \text{Knowledge Gap} \neq \text{Hypothesis} \neq \text{Discovery}.

EIM operates strictly between Levels 0 (Observation) and 4 (Knowledge Gap) of the evidence state model. It does not generate hypotheses or claim discoveries. Its output is a traceable, evidence-centered prioritization of regions for human investigation.

---

1. Motivation

1.1 The Synthesis Deficit

Mars is not an unexplored planet. It is an incompletely synthesized planet. The Planetary Data System (PDS) holds over 1.2 petabytes of planetary data, with growth rates that continue to accelerate. Two distinct bottlenecks constrain scientific return from this corpus:

(B1) Volume–capacity bottleneck. The raw volume of observations exceeds human inspection capacity:

\frac{D_{\text{Mars}}}{T_{\text{human}}} \gg H_{\text{human}},

where D_{\text{Mars}} is archival volume, T_{\text{human}} is the time available for human inspection, and H_{\text{human}} is aggregate human inspection throughput (bytes per unit time). This bottleneck motivates data-reduction and screening systems.

(B2) Relationship–capacity bottleneck. The number of possible pairwise cross-modal relationships grows quadratically in the number of modalities, and the number of possible higher-order intersections grows combinatorially. Even the pairwise case exceeds human synthesis capacity:

\binom{|\mathcal{M}|}{2} \gg H_{\text{human}},

where |\mathcal{M}| is the number of available observation modalities. With ten modalities, there are 45 pairwise relationships; with twenty, 190. If higher-order intersections are considered, the count grows as 2^{|\mathcal{M}|}-1, exceeding one million for twenty modalities. EIM focuses on pairwise relationships as a tractable and scientifically interpretable first-order approximation, while acknowledging that higher-order intersections are a natural extension. The operative bottleneck for EIM is B2: even if every observation were inspected, the relationship structure between observations would remain unsynthesized.

These are not refinements of one another; they are distinct inequalities with different units (bytes per time vs. modality-pair cardinality) and different operational implications.

1.2 The Operational Precedent

Autonomous targeting on Mars establishes that computational selection can increase scientific return. NASA/JPL's AEGIS system, first deployed on Opportunity and subsequently operational on Curiosity since May 2016, selected ChemCam targets with >93% success across 54 autonomous uses in its first operational year, compared to a 24% baseline without intelligent targeting. On Perseverance, AEGIS was deployed for SuperCam targeting in May 2022 (Sol 442), with over 150 autonomous targets selected as of the 2024 i-SAIRAS report.

However, AEGIS solves:

\text{Image} \rightarrow \text{Candidate Target} \rightarrow \text{Priority} \rightarrow \text{Observation}.

EIM addresses a different problem:

\text{Existing Observations} \rightarrow \text{Evidence Intersection} \rightarrow \text{Uncertainty/Gap Signals} \rightarrow \text{Candidate Scientific Attention}.

This distinction is architectural, not merely operational. AEGIS operates at or before the point of data acquisition. EIM operates after acquisition, on the accumulated evidence landscape.

