3. Core Research Questions

RQ1 (Retrieval). Can EIM recover known scientifically interesting regions from evidence profiles plus a disjoint literature index, without access to the held-out evaluation labels?

RQ2 (Ranking). Does EIM rank scientifically interesting regions above three baseline strategies: (a) publication density, (b) observation-footprint density, and (c) single-modality VAD scores?

RQ3 (Evidence Integrity). Can every candidate score be traced to explicit observations, metadata, and provenance chains with quantified uncertainty?

RQ4 (Ablation). Do the discrepancy term D(r) and gap term G(r) contribute ranking information beyond coverage C(r) and modality diversity M(r) alone?

Remark on RQ1. EIM consumes a literature index \mathcal{L} as a scoring input for G(r) (defined in §4.3). The evaluation labels used to score RQ1/RQ2 are independently generated human ratings (§6.1) and are never exposed to EIM. The distinction between scoring inputs and evaluation targets is maintained as a controlled variable throughout. Disjointness is enforced architecturally (§5.6) and re-asserted for the Oxia retrospective (§7).

