4. Formal Architecture

4.1 Evidence Objects

For a geographic region r \subseteq \mathcal{S} (where \mathcal{S} is the Martian surface), define an evidence set:

\mathcal{E}(r) = \{e_1, e_2, \ldots, e_n\}.

Each evidence object is a tuple:

e_i = \langle \text{id}, \ \text{loc}, \ \text{modality}, \ \text{timestamp}, \ \text{provenance}, \ \text{resolution}, \ \text{quality}, \ \text{uncertainty} \rangle,

where:

· \text{modality} \in \mathcal{M} = \{\text{CTX}, \text{HiRISE}, \text{DTM}, \text{CRISM}, \text{SHARAD}, \text{THEMIS}, \text{rover}, \ldots\};
· \text{provenance} encodes the data pipeline from raw observation to derived product;
· \text{uncertainty} is a probability distribution or interval over the observation value.

Literature is not an observation modality; it is handled separately via the literature index \mathcal{L} in §4.3.

4.2 Registration Confidence (First-Class Variable)

Co-registration error is a primary source of spurious cross-modal discrepancy. Published evidence documents offsets of 100 m horizontally and 10 m vertically between overlapping HiRISE DTMs; CTX geometric distortions of tens of meters that required rubber-sheet correction; and MOLA's 150 m footprint making direct CTX/HiRISE-vs-MOLA comparison likely to be meaningless in highly sloped areas. The global CTX mosaic construction reports that 6.4% of registrations required manual correction, with up to 70% of image pairs failing automatic registration in southern mid-latitudes.

We therefore introduce two registration objects with distinct types.

Pairwise registration matrix. For region r and modality pair (m_i, m_j) with spatial overlap in r:

R_{ij}(r) \in [0, 1],

derived from stereo-pair geometry residuals, DTM control residuals against MOLA/HRSC, tie-point error statistics, and published co-registration quality assessments.

Scalar reduction (well-typing). The evidence profile tuple \mathcal{P}(r) contains scalars; the pairwise matrix R_{ij}(r) does not fit this type. We therefore reduce it to a single scalar using the worst-pair operator:

R(r) := \begin{cases}
\min_{(i,j) \in V(r)} R_{ij}(r), & V(r) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & V(r) = \emptyset,
\end{cases}

where V(r) = \{(i,j) : m_i, m_j \text{ spatially overlap in } r\} is the set of overlapping modality pairs. The full matrix \{R_{ij}(r)\} is retained separately in the output schema (§5.5) and is available for audit; only its reduction enters \mathcal{P}(r). The choice of \min over \text{mean} is deliberate: registration failure on any overlapping pair can generate a spurious discrepancy, so the profile should reflect the weakest link.

Discrepancy gating. The discrepancy term D(r) is computed only over modality pairs satisfying:

R_{ij}(r) > \tau_R.

Regions where no pair satisfies the threshold report discrepancy status: unverifiable rather than a numeric score.

4.3 Evidence Profile Components

Let V_\tau(r) = \{(i,j) \in V(r) : R_{ij}(r) > \tau_R\} denote the registration-valid pairs in r.

Coverage C(r). Fraction of region area with at least one observation, weighted by observation quality. We define it on the union of footprints to avoid the counterintuitive behavior where adding a partial observation to a fully covered region lowers the score:

C(r) = \frac{1}{|r|} \int_r \max_{e:\, x \in \text{footprint}(e)} q(e) \, dx,

where q(e) \in [0,1] is the quality metadata of e. If no observation covers x, the integrand is 0. This definition is bounded in [0,1] and correctly rewards coverage.

Modality Diversity M(r). Normalized entropy of the modality distribution:

M(r) = -\frac{1}{\log |\mathcal{M}|} \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}} p_m(r) \log p_m(r),

where p_m(r) is the proportion of evidence objects of modality m in r.

Cross-Modal Agreement A(r). For comparable modalities (same physical quantity, similar resolution), the inverse of normalized divergence. Restricted to agreement-comparable pairs V_A(r) \subseteq V_\tau(r):

A(r) = \begin{cases}
1 - \frac{1}{|V_A(r)|} \sum_{(i,j) \in V_A(r)} \delta_{ij}(r), & V_A(r) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & V_A(r) = \emptyset,
\end{cases}

where \delta_{ij}(r) \in [0,1] is a normalized divergence between measurements from modalities i and j in r. The primitive \delta_{ij}(r) is defined as follows: after mapping both observations to a common physical quantity and unit (e.g., elevation, spectral index, thermal inertia), let P_i and P_j be the corresponding probability distributions (or intervals) over values in r. Then:

\delta_{ij}(r) = \frac{1}{2} \left( D_{\text{KL}}(P_i \| M) + D_{\text{KL}}(P_j \| M) \right),

where M = \frac{1}{2}(P_i + P_j) is the mixture distribution, and D_{\text{KL}} is the Kullback–Leibler divergence. This is the Jensen–Shannon divergence, which is symmetric, bounded in [0, \log 2], and can be normalized to [0,1] by dividing by \log 2. For scalar measurements with uncertainty intervals, we approximate P_i and P_j as Gaussian distributions centered on the measured values with standard deviations given by the uncertainty metadata. Pairs measuring incommensurable quantities (e.g., thermal inertia vs. albedo) are excluded from V_A and V_D.

Cross-Modal Discrepancy D(r) — aggregation rule. Discrepancy is defined pairwise at Level 3 of the evidence state model:

D(E_i, E_j) = \delta_{ij}(r), \quad (i,j) \in V_\tau(r),

but the profile requires a region-level scalar. We define:

D(r) = \begin{cases}
\frac{\sum_{(i,j) \in V_D(r)} w_{ij} \cdot \delta_{ij}(r)}{\sum_{(i,j) \in V_D(r)} w_{ij}}, & |V_D(r)| > 0, \\
\text{null}, & |V_D(r)| = 0,
\end{cases}

where:

· V_D(r) \subseteq V_\tau(r) is the set of discrepancy-comparable pairs — pairs measuring the same physical quantity (excluding known incommensurabilities);
· w_{ij} \geq 0 is a pair-weight reflecting modality independence. We replace subjective heuristics with a measurable independence metric:

w_{ij} = 1 - \text{Jaccard}(\text{calibration\_chain}_i, \text{calibration\_chain}_j),

where \text{calibration\_chain}_i is the list of dependencies extracted from the PDS4 label for observation i: [instrument, spacecraft clock, SPICE kernels, DTM control source]. The Jaccard index measures the overlap of these dependency sets. Pairs with completely independent chains receive w_{ij}=1; pairs sharing all dependencies receive w_{ij}=0. This is computable from metadata and avoids subjective judgment.

If |V_D(r)| = 0, the region reports D(r) = \text{null} with status unverifiable. We additionally report \max_{(i,j)} \delta_{ij}(r) as a secondary statistic, since a single large discrepancy may be scientifically more informative than a high mean over many small ones. Both statistics are exported; the primary scalar D(r) uses the weighted mean.

Evidence Quality Q(r). Aggregated quality metadata from PDS labels, including calibration level, data-usage constraints, and PDS3/PDS4 metadata completeness:

Q(r) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{E}(r)|} \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}(r)} q(e).

Unresolved Context G(r). Operationalized as the proportion of region covered by high-quality observations that lack corresponding published interpretation in a literature index \mathcal{L}, conditional on the region being within the spatial extent of at least one relevant publication's study area:

G(r) = \begin{cases}
\frac{\left|\{ x \in r : C_{\text{hq}}(x) > 0 \ \wedge \ x \notin \bigcup_{p \in \mathcal{L}} \text{study}(p) \}\right|}{\left|\{ x \in r : C_{\text{hq}}(x) > 0 \}\right|}, & \text{if } r \cap \bigcup_{p \in \mathcal{L}} \text{study}(p) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

where C_{\text{hq}}(x) indicates coverage by high-quality observations at location x, and \text{study}(p) is the spatial footprint of publication p. Regions with no literature context at all are handled by a separate uncovered flag U(r) \in \{0,1\}, where U(r)=1 if r has no overlapping publication in \mathcal{L}. This flag is used in S_{\text{followup}} (§4.4) to represent true terra incognita without assigning a numeric G(r).

Registration confidence (scalar entry). R(r) = \min_{(i,j) \in V(r)} R_{ij}(r) as defined in §4.2, with null if V(r)=\emptyset.

Evidence profile tuple. The full profile is therefore a well-typed vector:

\mathcal{P}(r) = \big(C(r), \ M(r), \ A(r), \ D(r), \ Q(r), \ G(r), \ R(r)\big) \in [0,1]^3 \times ([0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\})^4,

with the caveat that A(r), D(r), G(r), and R(r) may be null when the corresponding sets are empty. The pairwise matrix \{R_{ij}(r)\}, the pairwise discrepancy set \{\delta_{ij}(r)\}, and the set of contributing observations \mathcal{E}(r) are retained in the output schema for provenance export.

4.4 Multi-Objective Scoring

We replace a single additive score with a multi-objective framework:

\mathbf{S}(r) = \big(S_{\text{synthesis}}(r), \ S_{\text{followup}}(r), \ S_{\text{contradiction}}(r), \ S_{\text{anomaly}}(r)\big),

where each objective is a weighted combination of profile components with objective-specific weights:

S_{\text{synthesis}}(r) = f_{\text{syn}}\big(C, M, A, Q\big), \quad
S_{\text{followup}}(r) = f_{\text{fup}}\big(G, U, 1 - C, M\big),

S_{\text{contradiction}}(r) = f_{\text{con}}\big(D, R\big), \quad
S_{\text{anomaly}}(r) = f_{\text{ano}}\big(\Lambda(r)\big),

and \Lambda(r) is the local anomaly deviation (defined at Level 2 of the evidence state model; see §4.5). We use \Lambda rather than A to avoid collision with agreement A(r). \Lambda(r) is defined as an imported VAD score (e.g., PatchCore or PaDiM) computed on a single modality (typically CTX) for the region, with full provenance recorded. It is not computed by EIM itself.

Critical constraint. \mathbf{S}(r) \neq P(\text{discovery} \mid r). The score is a prioritization function, not a probability of discovery.

Ranking rule. To produce a ranked list for human inspection and for evaluation, we use a pre-registered scalarization with equal default weights:

S_{\text{final}}(r) = \frac{1}{4} \big( S_{\text{synthesis}}(r) + S_{\text{followup}}(r) + S_{\text{contradiction}}(r) + S_{\text{anomaly}}(r) \big).

The weights are user-configurable in the interface, but the default is fixed before evaluation. For evaluation, we also report Pareto fronts and dominated-set filtering to avoid reliance on a single scalarization.

4.5 Evidence State Model

Level State Formalization EIM Operates?
0 Observation O exists in archive Yes
1 Feature F(O) extracted Yes
2 Anomaly \Lambda(F) deviates from baseline Yes (secondary objective)
3 Discrepancy D(E_i, E_j), conditional on R_{ij} > \tau_R Yes (primary)
4 Knowledge Gap G(\mathcal{E}, Q) Yes (primary)
5 Hypothesis H(G) No
6 Discovery \text{Validation}(H, \mathcal{E}) No

EIM's primary contribution lies at Level 3 (Discrepancy) and Level 4 (Knowledge Gap), with Level 2 (Anomaly) as a secondary signal. The symbol \Lambda denotes local anomaly deviation; A(r) denotes cross-modal agreement and is unrelated.

