5. Minimal Prototype

5.1 Inputs

· CTX imagery (contextual imaging, ~6 m/pixel)
· HiRISE imagery (high-resolution, ~0.25–0.5 m/pixel)
· DTM products (HiRISE- and CTX-derived, with co-registration metadata)
· Geographic metadata (PDS4 labels, footprint vectors)
· Registration confidence layers (stereo residuals, control-point errors)
· ODE API for dataset discovery: https://ode.rsl.wustl.edu/mars/coverage/ODE_Mars_shapefile.html

5.2 Processing Pipeline

```
Dataset Discovery (ODE API query: CTX EDR + HiRISE RDR + DTM coverage)
        ↓
Spatial Indexing (hierarchical grid: 1 km, 5 km, 20 km)
        ↓
Registration Confidence Assessment (per-pair R_ij → scalar R_min)
        ↓
Observation Normalization (radiometric, geometric)
        ↓
Region Tiling (fixed grid + sliding window + feature-based)
        ↓
Evidence Aggregation → P(r) = (C, M, A, D, Q, G, R, U)
        ↓
Multi-Objective Scoring → S(r) ∈ ℝ⁴   [weights pre-registered]
        ↓
Scalarization → S_final(r)   [equal default weights]
        ↓
Candidate Ranking with Provenance Export
        ↓
Human Inspection Interface
```

5.3 Tiling Strategy

Implement and compare four tiling strategies:

1. Fixed hierarchical grid (1 km, 5 km, 20 km cells)
2. Sliding window (overlapping, scale-adaptive)
3. Region-growing from detected features (morphological boundaries)
4. Overlay with USGS geologic maps where available

Report ranking stability across tiling methods as a robustness check.

5.4 Weight Specification (Pre-Registration)

Weights \{w_{ij}\} inside D(r) are computed from the Jaccard independence metric (§4.3). The functional forms \{f_{\text{syn}}, f_{\text{fup}}, f_{\text{con}}, f_{\text{ano}}\} are linear combinations with equal default weights:

f_{\text{syn}} = \frac{1}{4}(C + M + A + Q), \quad
f_{\text{fup}} = \frac{1}{4}(G + U + (1-C) + M),

f_{\text{con}} = \frac{1}{2}(D + R), \quad
f_{\text{ano}} = \Lambda.

These coefficients are pre-registered before any ROI evaluation and reported in the output schema. No parameter tuning against ROI labels is performed. A sensitivity analysis over \pm 25\% perturbation of pre-registered weights is reported (§6.4).

5.5 Output Schema

```
CSAZ-XXXXXX
  Region ID
  Bounding coordinates
  Observation count
  Modalities represented
  Spatial resolution range
  Temporal coverage range
  Evidence profile: (C, M, A, D, Q, G, R, U)
  Discrepancy status: {verified, unverifiable, none}
  Pairwise registration matrix: {R_ij(r)}
  Pairwise discrepancy set: {δ_ij(r)}
  Objective scores: (S_synthesis, S_followup, S_contradiction, S_anomaly)
  Scalarized score: S_final
  Weight provenance: {w_ij, f_* coefficients, version, pre-registration hash}
  Literature disjointness audit: {L ∩ L_ROI = ∅ at claim level, geographic exclusion applied, temporal exclusion applied}
  Existing interpretation: {cited publications}
  Provenance chain: {observation IDs, processing steps, parameters}
  Status: Candidate Scientific Attention Zone
  NOT: Discovery / Confirmed anomaly / Geological interpretation
```

5.6 Label/Input Disjointness (Controlled Variable)

To ensure RQ1 is a falsifiable claim, the following sets are maintained disjoint:

· \mathcal{L} — literature index used in G(r);
· \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}} — publication set from which evaluation ROI candidates are drawn;
· \mathcal{Y} — human-rated evaluation labels (§6.1).

Disjointness is enforced at the level of individual interpretation claims. Two-stage filter:

1. Geographic exclusion: any publication whose study footprint overlaps an ROI by more than 10% is removed from \mathcal{L} for that ROI's G(r) computation.
2. Temporal exclusion: for all ROIs, a literature entry must have timestamp earlier than the ROI's defining publication timestamp minus one year.

Enforcement is auditable via the output schema's literature disjointness audit field.

