6. Evaluation Strategy

6.1 Labeled Evaluation Set Construction

No validated corpus of "known scientifically interesting regions" with boundaries exists. Construct one:

1. Query ADS/LPSC/EPSC abstracts (2000–2026) for Mars regions with explicit geographic focus, yielding \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}}.
2. Extract region-of-interest (ROI) boundaries from figures, maps, or textual descriptions.
3. Select 100–300 ROIs stratified by modality coverage, latitude, terrain type, and publication year.
4. Recruit \geq 2 planetary scientists to independently rate each ROI on:
   · scientific importance (Likert 1–5);
   · evidence richness (Likert 1–5);
   · priority for follow-up inspection (Likert 1–5);
   · whether the evidence configuration itself warrants inspection (Likert 1–5).
5. Report inter-rater agreement using quadratic-weighted Cohen's \kappa and intraclass correlation coefficient ICC(2,k). Retain ROIs with \kappa \geq 0.6.
6. For power analysis: with 100–300 ROIs and a 5-point Likert scale, we can detect a difference of 0.5 points with 80% power at \alpha=0.05 for the primary comparisons.

6.2 Baselines

Baseline Description Purpose
B1: Publication density Count of publications per region Tests whether EIM learns "more literature = more interesting"
B2: Footprint density Count of observation footprints per region Tests whether EIM learns "more data = more interesting"
B3: Single-modality VAD PatchCore or PaDiM anomaly score on CTX tiles only (fixed 6 m scale) Tests whether EIM adds beyond pixel-level anomaly detection
B4: Random Randomly sampled regions Floor

For B3, we report both IMG-ROC and PRO (per-region overlap) metrics as used in VAD literature.

6.3 Negative Controls (Stratified)

1. Observation-dense, scientifically unremarkable regions (stratified by latitude, terrain type, epoch; following the thermal-inertia partitioning strategy of the fresh-crater work)
2. Visually unusual regions with well-established explanations (e.g., known aeolian features)
3. Regions with intentionally incomplete evidence (partial coverage, missing modalities)
4. Regions with known scientific importance (positive control)
5. Randomly sampled regions (negative control)

6.4 Ablation Study (H2 Test)

Compare ranking performance of:

· Full model: S_{\text{final}}(C, M, A, D, Q, G, R, U)
· No-D/G model: S_{\text{final}}(C, M, A, Q, R, U)
· No-R model: S_{\text{final}}(C, M, A, D, Q, G, U)
· Coverage-only: C
· Diversity-only: M
· Sensitivity variants: full model under \pm 25\% perturbation of each pre-registered weight

If removing D and G does not degrade ranking performance, H2 is falsified and the contribution reduces to suitability MCDM (§2.4). Ranking performance is reported via precision@k (with k=10, 50, 100), NDCG@k, and AUC against the held-out labels \mathcal{Y}.

6.5 Traceability Rubric

Level Description
0 No provenance
1 Data source identified
2 Specific observation identified (ID, timestamp)
3 Transformation pipeline documented
4 Uncertainty quantified
5 Full reproducibility (code, parameters, data versions)

Target: Level 4+ for all candidates. Report

\text{Traceability} = \frac{\text{candidates at Level} \geq 4}{\text{total candidates}}.

If a PDS label lacks uncertainty, set q(e)=0.5 and flag as "uncertainty: unknown" — do not invent numbers.

