7. Case Study: Oxia Planum Retrospective (Leakage-Controlled)

Replace the Olympus Mons example with a retrospective case study on Oxia Planum.

Why Oxia Planum.

· Documented manual cross-modal fusion (SHARAD + CTX MADNet DTM + CRISM)
· Explicitly stated need for automation
· Concrete discrepancy story: possible subsurface layers vs. clutter ambiguity, unresolved after cluttergram comparison
· Known scientific importance (ExoMars landing site)

Retrospective Protocol (Leakage-Controlled).

1. Define a temporal cutoff T_{\text{cut}} = 2018\text{-}01\text{-}01, the date before which the observations studied by Xiong et al. (2023) were available.
2. Construct the evidence set \mathcal{E}(r) using only observations with timestamp < T_{\text{cut}}.
3. Construct the literature index \mathcal{L} using only interpretations published < T_{\text{cut}}. Additionally, apply the two-stage filter: exclude any publication whose study footprint overlaps Oxia Planum by more than 10%, and exclude any publication within one year of the Xiong et al. study.
4. Apply EIM scoring to Oxia Planum using \mathcal{E}(r) and \mathcal{L} as restricted above.
5. Compare EIM CSAZ output with the region actually studied by Xiong et al. (2023).
6. Report: Would EIM have surfaced the same candidate zone, with traceable evidence, before the manual study was conducted?

Falsifiability condition (concrete). H4 is falsified if, under the T_{\text{cut}} restriction on both observations and literature index, and under the two-stage geographic/temporal exclusion, the Xiong et al. (2023) study region is not in the top-5% of CSAZs by S_{\text{contradiction}} within a 50 km radius of Oxia Planum. The 50 km radius corresponds to the landing ellipse scale. If the restriction is lifted on \mathcal{L} alone, the test is circular and H4 is not evaluated.

---

8. Failure Modes (Explicit and Architectural)

Failure Mode Mitigation
Observation-density bias Include G(r) and U(r); normalize C(r) by global coverage distribution
Instrument bias Weight modalities by Jaccard independence; report per-modality contribution
Resolution bias Multi-scale tiling; scale-space representations; report scale-dependence
Archive bias Include G(r); stratify negative controls by archive completeness
Publication bias Use G(r) conditional on publication spatial extent; do not use publication density as target; enforce \mathcal{L} \cap \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}} = \emptyset
False discrepancy R(r) threshold; exclude known incommensurabilities; report "unverifiable" status
Novelty inflation Literature cross-check for all high-D candidates; report existing interpretations
Registration artifact R(r) as first-class variable; propagate co-registration uncertainty
Weight overfitting Pre-registered weights; held-out ROI test set; sensitivity analysis
Retrospective leakage Temporal cutoff T_{\text{cut}} on both observations and literature index; two-stage geographic/temporal exclusion

---

9. Hypotheses

H1 (Primary). A geographically localized evidence-intersection representation combining heterogeneous existing Mars observations can identify Candidate Scientific Attention Zones more effectively than single-modality observation ranking, while maintaining explicit evidence provenance.

H2 (Ablation). Explicit representation of cross-modal discrepancy D(r) and unresolved context G(r) identifies scientifically relevant candidates that would be missed by ranking based solely on coverage C(r) or modality diversity M(r).

H3 (Registration). Incorporating registration confidence R(r) as a first-class variable reduces false-discrepancy rates relative to naive cross-modal comparison.

H4 (Retrospective, leakage-controlled). EIM, applied to pre-2018 Oxia Planum data with a literature index also truncated at T_{\text{cut}} = 2018\text{-}01\text{-}01 and with two-stage geographic/temporal exclusion, would have surfaced the region subsequently studied by Xiong et al. (2023) as a high-priority CSAZ, specifically within the top-5% of CSAZs by S_{\text{contradiction}} within a 50 km radius.

All hypotheses are empirically testable and falsifiable.

---

10. Scope Boundary

This project deliberately does not attempt to:

· build a general Mars crawler,
· replace planetary scientists,
· autonomously publish scientific conclusions,
· claim extraterrestrial discoveries,
· generate unsupported geological hypotheses,
· operate spacecraft,
· perform visual anomaly detection as a primary objective,
· or build a complete planetary foundation model.

The first objective is:

Build a reliable, traceable evidence-intersection component and determine whether it can help humans find scientifically interesting places in existing Mars data.

---

11. Expected Contributions

1. Formal evidence-state ontology with explicit uncertainty quantification at Levels 0–4, well-typed at the profile level.
2. Multi-objective evidence-intersection scoring framework separating synthesis, follow-up, contradiction, and anomaly objectives, with a pre-registered scalarization for ranking.
3. Registration-confidence-aware cross-modal discrepancy computation with a computable independence metric for pair weights and documented aggregation rule.
4. Provenance-preserving CSAZ generation with traceability rubric and Level 4+ target.
5. Leakage-controlled retrospective validation protocol on Oxia Planum, grounded in a published manual-fusion workflow, with concrete falsifiability condition.
6. Stratified negative-control methodology for planetary evidence-ranking evaluation, incorporating thermal-inertia-style confounding-variable partitioning.
7. Pre-registered, non-fitted weight specification compatible with held-out evaluation on a small ROI sample.
8. Modular architecture extensible to CRISM, SHARAD, rover, and literature modalities.

---

12. Longer-Term Vision

If validated, EIM becomes a reusable component within a broader evidence-constrained scientific discovery architecture:

\boxed{\text{Evidence} \rightarrow \text{Intersection} \rightarrow \text{Discrepancy} \rightarrow \text{Gap} \rightarrow \text{Question}}

For Mars:

\text{Mars Data} \rightarrow \text{EIM} \rightarrow \text{CSAZs} \rightarrow \text{Scientific Questions} \rightarrow \text{Targeted Observations}

The long-term objective is not a "Mars AI." It is to investigate whether evidence intersection can function as a computational primitive for scientific problem discovery. Mars is the first experimental environment.

---

13. Conclusion

Mars has been extensively observed but incompletely synthesized. Existing autonomous systems demonstrate that machines can select targets for observation. Existing GIS tools enable human-driven multi-dataset visualization. Existing ML systems perform single-modality anomaly detection. No lightweight, provenance-preserving component computes region-level evidence profiles across heterogeneous archives to rank candidate zones for human investigation.

EIM addresses this gap. It asks not "What can the machine discover?" but "Where does the existing evidence indicate that we should look more carefully?" The Oxia Planum retrospective, under an explicit temporal cutoff on both observations and literature index, and with a concrete top-5% within 50 km falsifiability condition, provides a falsifiable validation pathway. The multi-objective scoring framework, registration-confidence architecture, well-typed profile, pre-registered weight specification, and stratified evaluation protocol address the principal weaknesses of earlier drafts.

If EIM works, it becomes one small puzzle piece in a larger architecture of evidence-constrained scientific discovery — a component that makes the existing scientific evidence landscape more searchable, without pretending to replace the scientists who interpret it.

