Appendix B: Boundedness and Well-Typing Proofs

B.1 Coverage C(r) \in [0,1]

Definition.

C(r) = \frac{1}{|r|} \int_r f(x) \, dx,

where

f(x) = \begin{cases}
\max_{e:\, x \in \text{footprint}(e)} q(e), & \{e : x \in \text{footprint}(e)\} \neq \emptyset, \\
0, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Proof.

For each x \in r, f(x) is either 0 or the maximum of a finite set of values q(e) \in [0,1]. Therefore f(x) \in [0,1] for all x \in r.

Since f is bounded and measurable (footprints are polygons, q is piecewise constant), the integral satisfies:

0 \leq \int_r f(x) \, dx \leq \int_r 1 \, dx = |r|.

Dividing by |r| > 0:

0 \leq C(r) \leq 1. \quad \blacksquare

Remark. This definition avoids the counterintuitive behavior of the earlier quality-weighted mean formula, where adding a partial observation to a fully covered region could decrease C(r). The union-based definition correctly rewards coverage.

B.2 Modality Diversity M(r) \in [0,1]

Definition.

M(r) = -\frac{1}{\log |\mathcal{M}|} \sum_{m \in \mathcal{M}} p_m(r) \log p_m(r),

where

p_m(r) = \frac{|\{e \in \mathcal{E}(r) : \text{modality}(e) = m\}|}{|\mathcal{E}(r)|}.

Proof.

Assume |\mathcal{M}| > 1 (in practice |\mathcal{M}| \geq 7). By Gibbs' inequality, for any probability distribution p:

-\sum_m p_m \log p_m \geq 0,

with equality iff p_m = 1 for some m. By the maximum entropy principle:

-\sum_m p_m \log p_m \leq \log |\mathcal{M}|,

with equality iff p_m = 1/|\mathcal{M}| for all m. Dividing by \log |\mathcal{M}| > 0:

0 \leq M(r) \leq 1. \quad \blacksquare

Edge case. If \mathcal{E}(r) = \emptyset, define M(r) = 0 (no evidence, no diversity).

B.3 Cross-Modal Agreement A(r) \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}

Definition.

A(r) = \begin{cases}
1 - \frac{1}{|V_A(r)|} \sum_{(i,j) \in V_A(r)} \delta_{ij}(r), & V_A(r) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & V_A(r) = \emptyset.
\end{cases}

Proof.

Assume V_A(r) \neq \emptyset. Each \delta_{ij}(r) \in [0,1] (proved in Appendix D). Therefore:

0 \leq \frac{1}{|V_A(r)|} \sum_{(i,j) \in V_A(r)} \delta_{ij}(r) \leq 1.

Subtracting from 1:

0 \leq A(r) \leq 1. \quad \blacksquare

B.4 Cross-Modal Discrepancy D(r) \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}

Definition.

D(r) = \begin{cases}
\frac{\sum_{(i,j) \in V_D(r)} w_{ij} \cdot \delta_{ij}(r)}{\sum_{(i,j) \in V_D(r)} w_{ij}}, & |V_D(r)| > 0, \\
\text{null}, & |V_D(r)| = 0.
\end{cases}

Proof.

Assume |V_D(r)| > 0. Each w_{ij} \geq 0 and \delta_{ij}(r) \in [0,1]. Since at least one w_{ij} > 0 (otherwise the denominator is zero, contradiction), the numerator is a non-negative weighted sum of values in [0,1], and the denominator is a positive weighted sum. Therefore D(r) is a convex combination of values in [0,1]:

0 \leq D(r) \leq 1. \quad \blacksquare

B.5 Evidence Quality Q(r) \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}

Definition.

Q(r) = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{E}(r)|} \sum_{e \in \mathcal{E}(r)} q(e).

Proof.

Assume \mathcal{E}(r) \neq \emptyset. Each q(e) \in [0,1], so the average is in [0,1]. If \mathcal{E}(r) = \emptyset, define Q(r) = \text{null}. \blacksquare

B.6 Unresolved Context G(r) \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}

Definition.

Let

N(r) = \{x \in r : C_{\text{hq}}(x) > 0 \ \wedge \ x \notin \bigcup_{p \in \mathcal{L}} \text{study}(p)\},

D_G(r) = \{x \in r : C_{\text{hq}}(x) > 0\}.

Then

G(r) = \begin{cases}
\frac{|N(r)|}{|D_G(r)|}, & |D_G(r)| > 0 \ \wedge \ r \cap \bigcup_{p \in \mathcal{L}} \text{study}(p) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Proof.

Assume the conditions for the non-null case hold. By construction, N(r) \subseteq D_G(r), since every x \in N(r) satisfies C_{\text{hq}}(x) > 0. Therefore |N(r)| \leq |D_G(r)|, and since |D_G(r)| > 0:

0 \leq G(r) \leq 1. \quad \blacksquare

B.7 Registration Confidence R(r) \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}

Definition.

R(r) = \begin{cases}
\min_{(i,j) \in V(r)} R_{ij}(r), & V(r) \neq \emptyset, \\
\text{null}, & V(r) = \emptyset.
\end{cases}

Proof.

Assume V(r) \neq \emptyset. Each R_{ij}(r) \in [0,1], so the minimum is in [0,1]. \blacksquare

B.8 Well-Typing of the Profile Tuple

Claim.

\mathcal{P}(r) = \big(C(r), M(r), A(r), D(r), Q(r), G(r), R(r)\big) \in [0,1]^3 \times ([0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\})^4.

Proof.

From B.1, B.2, B.5: C, M, Q \in [0,1] when \mathcal{E}(r) \neq \emptyset. If \mathcal{E}(r) = \emptyset, C = 0, M = 0, Q = \text{null}. For the purposes of the type signature, we treat Q = \text{null} as the nullable case.

From B.3, B.4, B.6, B.7: A, D, G, R \in [0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\}.

Therefore the tuple type is [0,1]^3 \times ([0,1] \cup \{\text{null}\})^4. \blacksquare

