Appendix D: Jensen–Shannon Divergence and \delta_{ij} Derivation

D.1 Definition

For two probability distributions P and Q over a common measurable space, the Jensen–Shannon divergence is:

\text{JS}(P \| Q) = \frac{1}{2} D_{\text{KL}}(P \| M) + \frac{1}{2} D_{\text{KL}}(Q \| M),

where M = \frac{1}{2}(P + Q) is the mixture distribution and

D_{\text{KL}}(P \| M) = \int p(x) \log \frac{p(x)}{m(x)} \, dx

is the Kullback–Leibler divergence.

D.2 Properties

1. Non-negativity: \text{JS}(P \| Q) \geq 0, with equality iff P = Q.
2. Symmetry: \text{JS}(P \| Q) = \text{JS}(Q \| P).
3. Boundedness: \text{JS}(P \| Q) \leq \log 2.
4. Metric property: \sqrt{\text{JS}(P \| Q)} is a metric.

D.3 Normalization

Define the normalized divergence:

\delta_{ij}(r) = \frac{\text{JS}(P_i \| P_j)}{\log 2}.

By property D.2.3, \delta_{ij}(r) \in [0,1]. \blacksquare

D.4 Gaussian Approximation for Scalar Measurements

When measurements are scalar with uncertainty intervals, we approximate:

P_i \approx \mathcal{N}(\mu_i, \sigma_i^2), \quad P_j \approx \mathcal{N}(\mu_j, \sigma_j^2),

where \mu_i is the measured value and \sigma_i is the reported uncertainty. The JS divergence between two Gaussians has no closed form, but can be computed numerically or approximated by:

\text{JS}(P_i \| P_j) \approx \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{(\mu_i - \mu_j)^2}{\sigma_i^2 + \sigma_j^2} + \frac{(\sigma_i^2 - \sigma_j^2)^2}{(\sigma_i^2 + \sigma_j^2)^2} \right) \cdot \frac{1}{2},

for small divergence. This is an approximation; exact computation is performed numerically.

D.5 Handling Incommensurable Quantities

Pairs measuring incommensurable quantities (e.g., thermal inertia vs. albedo) are excluded from V_A and V_D. Formally, for each modality m, we define a physical quantity label \phi(m) \in \{\text{elevation}, \text{reflectance}, \text{thermal}, \text{radar}, \ldots\}. Then:

V_A(r) = \{(i,j) \in V_\tau(r) : \phi(m_i) = \phi(m_j) \ \wedge \ \text{resolution}(m_i) \approx \text{resolution}(m_j)\}.

The resolution condition is enforced by requiring |\log_{10}(\text{res}_i / \text{res}_j)| < 0.5 (i.e., within a factor of ~3).

---

Appendix E: Jaccard Independence Metric for Pair Weights

E.1 Calibration Chain Definition

For each observation e_i, the calibration chain is a set:

C_i = \{\text{instrument}_i, \ \text{spacecraft}_i, \ \text{SPICE}_i, \ \text{DTM\_control}_i\},

extracted from the PDS4 label. Each element is a string identifier.

E.2 Jaccard Similarity

\text{Jaccard}(A, B) = \frac{|A \cap B|}{|A \cup B|}, \quad A \cup B \neq \emptyset.

If A \cup B = \emptyset, define \text{Jaccard}(A, B) = 0 (no shared dependencies known).

E.3 Pair Weight

w_{ij} = 1 - \text{Jaccard}(C_i, C_j).

E.4 Boundedness Proof

Since \text{Jaccard}(C_i, C_j) \in [0,1], we have w_{ij} \in [0,1]. \blacksquare

E.5 Worked Example

For CTX and HiRISE:

C_{\text{CTX}} = \{\text{CTX}, \text{MRO}, \text{SPICE\_MRO}, \text{MOLA}\},

C_{\text{HiRISE}} = \{\text{HiRISE}, \text{MRO}, \text{SPICE\_MRO}, \text{MOLA}\}.

|C_{\text{CTX}} \cap C_{\text{HiRISE}}| = 3, \quad |C_{\text{CTX}} \cup C_{\text{HiRISE}}| = 5,

\text{Jaccard} = 0.6, \quad w_{\text{CTX,HiRISE}} = 0.4.

For CTX and THEMIS:

C_{\text{THEMIS}} = \{\text{THEMIS}, \text{Odyssey}, \text{SPICE\_ODY}, \text{MOLA}\},

|C_{\text{CTX}} \cap C_{\text{THEMIS}}| = 1, \quad |C_{\text{CTX}} \cup C_{\text{THEMIS}}| = 7,

\text{Jaccard} = 1/7 \approx 0.143, \quad w_{\text{CTX,THEMIS}} \approx 0.857.

This correctly assigns higher independence weight to cross-spacecraft pairs.

