Appendix F: Two-Stage Literature Disjointness Filter

F.1 Geographic Exclusion

For each ROI r and each publication p \in \mathcal{L}:

\text{If } \frac{|\text{footprint}(p) \cap r|}{|r|} > 0.10, \text{ remove } p \text{ from } \mathcal{L} \text{ for } r.

This ensures that publications whose primary study area overlaps the ROI cannot contribute to G(r) as "context."

F.2 Temporal Exclusion

For each ROI r with defining publication p_r \in \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}}:

\text{If } \text{timestamp}(p) > \text{timestamp}(p_r) - 1 \text{ year}, \text{ remove } p \text{ from } \mathcal{L} \text{ for } r.

This prevents anachronistic leakage: publications that post-date the ROI's discovery cannot be used to establish "context" for that ROI.

F.3 Combined Filter

\mathcal{L}(r) = \{p \in \mathcal{L} : \text{geographic condition holds} \ \wedge \ \text{temporal condition holds}\}.

The disjointness requirement is:

\mathcal{L}(r) \cap \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}} = \emptyset \quad \text{at the level of individual interpretation claims.}

F.4 Auditability

The output schema records:

· The set \mathcal{L}(r) actually used for G(r);
· The set of publications excluded by geographic filter;
· The set of publications excluded by temporal filter;
· The hash of the full \mathcal{L} and \mathcal{L}_{\text{ROI}} sets.

