Appendix J: Computational Complexity Analysis

J.1 Region Tiling

For a study area of A km² with tile size s km:

N_{\text{tiles}} = O(A / s^2).

For hierarchical tiling with levels s_1, s_2, s_3:

N_{\text{tiles}} = O(A / s_1^2 + A / s_2^2 + A / s_3^2).

J.2 Evidence Aggregation

For each region r, computing C, M, Q requires iterating over \mathcal{E}(r):

T_C = O(|\mathcal{E}(r)|).

J.3 Pairwise Comparisons

For each region r, computing pairwise registration, agreement, and discrepancy requires iterating over modality pairs:

T_{\text{pairs}} = O(|\mathcal{M}|^2).

J.4 Total Complexity

T_{\text{total}} = O\left(N_{\text{tiles}} \cdot (|\mathcal{E}(r)| + |\mathcal{M}|^2)\right).

For N_{\text{tiles}} = 100{,}000, |\mathcal{M}| = 10, |\mathcal{E}(r)| \approx 100:

T_{\text{total}} \approx 100{,}000 \cdot (100 + 100) = 2 \times 10^7 \text{ operations}.

This is tractable on a single machine.

J.5 Pareto Front Computation

For m=4 objectives and N regions, the Pareto front can be computed in O(N \log^{m-1} N) time using divide-and-conquer. For m=4, this is O(N \log^3 N), which is tractable for N = 100{,}000.

---

Appendix K: Weight Pre-Registration Protocol

K.1 Pre-Registered Quantities

1. Pair weights w_{ij} computed from Jaccard metric (Appendix E).
2. Functional forms f_{\text{syn}}, f_{\text{fup}}, f_{\text{con}}, f_{\text{ano}} as linear combinations with equal default weights.
3. Scalarization weights \alpha = \beta = \gamma = \delta = 0.25.
4. Registration threshold \tau_R = 0.5.
5. Resolution comparability threshold |\log_{10}(\text{res}_i / \text{res}_j)| < 0.5.

K.2 Registration Mechanism

· Deposit the exact functional forms and coefficients in a timestamped repository (OSF, Zenodo).
· Record the SHA-256 hash of the pre-registration document.
· Include the hash in the output schema's weight_provenance field.
· No modification after evaluation begins.

K.3 Sensitivity Analysis

Report ranking stability under \pm 25\% perturbation of each pre-registered weight. This characterizes robustness without compromising held-out validity.

---

Appendix L: Tiling Strategy Formalization

L.1 Fixed Hierarchical Grid

For each level \ell \in \{1, 2, 3\} with cell size s_\ell:

\mathcal{T}_\ell = \{r_{i,j} : r_{i,j} = [i \cdot s_\ell, (i+1) \cdot s_\ell] \times [j \cdot s_\ell, (j+1) \cdot s_\ell]\}.

L.2 Sliding Window

For window size s and stride \Delta < s:

\mathcal{T}_{\text{slide}} = \{r_{i,j} : r_{i,j} = [i \cdot \Delta, i \cdot \Delta + s] \times [j \cdot \Delta, j \cdot \Delta + s]\}.

L.3 Region-Growing

Start from detected features (e.g., craters, valleys) and grow regions using morphological operations until boundaries are reached.

L.4 USGS Overlay

Intersect the fixed grid with USGS geologic map units:

\mathcal{T}_{\text{USGS}} = \{r \cap g : r \in \mathcal{T}_1, \ g \in \mathcal{G}_{\text{USGS}}\}.

L.5 Stability Metric

For two tiling strategies \mathcal{T}_A and \mathcal{T}_B, define:

\text{Stability}(\mathcal{T}_A, \mathcal{T}_B) = \frac{|\text{Top}_k(\mathcal{T}_A) \cap \text{Top}_k(\mathcal{T}_B)|}{k}.

Report for all pairs of strategies.

---

Appendix M: Stratified Negative Control Formalization

M.1 Strata Definition

Define strata by the Cartesian product:

\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{L} \times \mathcal{T} \times \mathcal{E} \times \mathcal{A},

where:

· \mathcal{L} = \{\text{polar}, \text{mid-latitude}, \text{equatorial}\} (latitude bands);
· \mathcal{T} = \{\text{plains}, \text{cratered}, \text{volcanic}, \text{sedimentary}\} (terrain type);
· \mathcal{E} = \{\text{Noachian}, \text{Hesperian}, \text{Amazonian}\} (geologic epoch);
· \mathcal{A} = \{\text{high}, \text{medium}, \text{low}\} (archive completeness).

M.2 Sampling

For each stratum s \in \mathcal{S}, sample n_s negative control regions, where:

n_s = n_{\text{total}} \cdot \frac{|\mathcal{R}_s|}{\sum_{s' \in \mathcal{S}} |\mathcal{R}_{s'}|},

and \mathcal{R}_s is the set of all regions in stratum s.

M.3 Thermal Inertia Partitioning

Following the fresh-crater work, partition candidates by thermal inertia (TI) values into bins:

\text{TI bins} = \{[0, 100), [100, 200), [200, 300), [300, \infty)\} \text{ J m}^{-2} \text{K}^{-1} \text{s}^{-1/2}.

Within each TI bin, sample negative controls proportionally. This reduces observational bias.

---

Appendix N: Limitations of the Mathematical Framework

N.1 Gaussian Approximation for \delta_{ij}

The Gaussian approximation for scalar measurements assumes symmetric uncertainty. For asymmetric uncertainties, the approximation may over- or under-estimate divergence. Future work should extend to skewed distributions.

N.2 Jaccard Metric Assumptions

The Jaccard metric assumes calibration chains are well-documented in PDS4 labels. If labels are incomplete, the metric may misestimate independence. Mitigation: flag observations with incomplete calibration metadata and set w_{ij} = 0.5 (neutral) for pairs involving such observations.

N.3 Linear Scoring Functions

The linear functional forms f_{\text{syn}}, f_{\text{fup}}, f_{\text{con}}, f_{\text{ano}} assume independence between profile components. If components are correlated (e.g., C and M), the linear combination may over-weight redundant information. Future work should explore non-linear combinations.

N.4 Pairwise Approach

The pairwise approach ignores higher-order interactions (triples, quadruples). A region where three modalities agree pairwise but disagree jointly would not be detected. Future work should extend to higher-order intersections.

N.5 Temporal Cutoff Reliability

The Oxia retrospective assumes timestamps are reliable. If observations have ambiguous or missing timestamps, the T_{\text{cut}} filter may be incorrect. Mitigation: exclude observations with missing timestamps and report the proportion excluded.

---

Appendix O: Proof of Total Function Property

O.1 Claim

The evidence profile \mathcal{P}(r) and the scalarized score S_{\text{final}}(r) are total functions: they are defined for every region r \subseteq \mathcal{S}.

O.2 Proof

Step 1: Profile totality. From Appendix C, every component of \mathcal{P}(r) has a defined value (either in [0,1] or null) for every region r. Therefore \mathcal{P}(r) is total.

Step 2: Scoring totality. The scalarized score is:

S_{\text{final}}(r) = \frac{1}{4} \left( \tilde{S}_{\text{syn}} + \tilde{S}_{\text{fup}} + \tilde{S}_{\text{con}} + \tilde{S}_{\text{ano}} \right),

where \tilde{x} = x if x \neq \text{null}, else 0. Since each \tilde{S}_k \in [0,1], S_{\text{final}} \in [0,1] for all r. Therefore S_{\text{final}} is total. \blacksquare

O.3 Corollary

The ranking of regions by S_{\text{final}} is well-defined for any finite set of regions.

